mary><category term="Culture" /></entry><entry><title>Germany news: Merz rails against extremism, AfD sympathizers</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:76e9740ee202a0d1cb0edd8dcd7bd67b89612d142a37460a569077e270d95277</id><updated>2026-10-08T06:19:28Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T15:17:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/germany-news-merz-rails-against-extremism-afd-sympathizers/live-79570924--74b91e3a40aa.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/germany-news-merz-rails-against-extremism-afd-sympathizers/live-79570924?maca=en-rss-en-top-1022-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Political extremism threatens Germany's existing social order, Chancellor Merz has said. The Transport Ministry has proposed a five-year plan to improve Germany's ailing railroad infrastructure.</summary><category term="News" /></entry><entry><title>EU enlargement: European Commission tables reforms to prepare bloc for new members</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b471e4357cd1db59dec20a05340e8a6d2a73a2393bc23eedd0e3d298456fce99</id><updated>2026-10-08T06:19:28Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T15:05:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/eu-enlargement-european-commission-tables-reforms-to-prepare-bloc-for-new-members/a-79582493--0237e634d1b1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/eu-enlargement-european-commission-tables-reforms-to-prepare-bloc-for-new-members/a-79582493?maca=en-rss-en-top-1022-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The European Commission is proposing earlier access to EU markets for candidate countries, new voting rules to reduce vetoes and extra safeguards for a bigger bloc. But is the reform fair — or even feasible?</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>China's Golden Week reveals cautious consumer spending despite travel surge</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5e57accb775201438113d08fcfca095e2c994d6844607ed325e44afd744498a9</id><updated>2026-10-08T06:19:28Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T13:18:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/china-s-golden-week-reveals-cautious-consumer-spending-despite-travel-surge/a-79577254--362469c231fc.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/china-s-golden-week-reveals-cautious-consumer-spending-despite-travel-surge/a-79577254?maca=en-rss-en-top-1022-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Chinese visitors are returning from trips after the 13-day Golden Week holiday. Their subdued spending has raised fresh questions about domestic demand in the world's second-largest economy.</summary><category term="World" /></entry><entry><title>German spy scandal threatens national security</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a1de5813df3a03568a67bf1755db193dc08d463a44972556b39eb4d9ec6c059b</id><updated>2026-10-08T06:19:28Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T12:39:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/german-spy-scandal-threatens-national-security/a-79580000--1e3ca23b32e3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/german-spy-scandal-threatens-national-security/a-79580000?maca=en-rss-en-top-1022-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">The arrest of Germany's former intelligence chief August Hanning could undermine national security, experts warn, as allies may reconsider their intelligence relationships.</summary><category term="Germany" /></entry><entry><title>France suspends use of stun grenades against protesters</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9bba529a7e182d8f60c2c55ab3eb15b01881625ee020929140b39c6f053855ea</id><updated>2026-10-08T06:19:28Z</updated><published>2026-10-07T12:12:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/france-suspends-use-of-stun-grenades-against-protesters/a-79577769--d0b3dc0a1c76.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/france-suspends-use-of-stun-grenades-against-protesters/a-79577769?maca=en-rss-en-top-1022-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">France suspended the use of stun grenades against protesters as allegations of student brutality draw more anger. French Prime Minister Sebastien Lecornu said the country could not ignore students' concerns.</summary><category term="News" /></entry></feed>
